<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.berwilson.cron-meeting-prep</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/zsh</string>
    <string>-c</string>
    <string>curl -sS -m 280 -H "Authorization: Bearer $(grep '^CRON_SECRET=' /Users/richardwhite/berwilson-platform/.env.local | cut -d= -f2-)" http://localhost:3000/api/cron/meeting-prep</string>
  </array>
  <!--
    Every 15 minutes, 07:00-18:00 on weekdays — the pre-meeting nudge.

    ⚠ THIS IS THE ONE CRON THAT MAY SHARE A SLOT WITH THE OTHERS, AND THE REASON
    IS STRUCTURAL. Every other job here is scheduled around LM Studio, which
    serves one request at a time with no queue anywhere in the codebase. This
    pass makes NO MODEL CALL AT ALL: everything it sends is already a fact — a
    name, a last-contact date, an open obligation — so it cannot sit in front of
    the agent or delay the digest. If that ever stops being true, this schedule
    is wrong and must move.

    WHY 15 MINUTES. The route looks 35 minutes ahead and skips anything closer
    than 5, so a 15-minute tick means every meeting is caught exactly once with
    roughly 20-35 minutes of warning. A longer interval would miss meetings; a
    shorter one would re-ask Google for the same calendar for nothing.

    Idempotency is the calendar EVENT, not the day — notification_log's
    dedupe_key, with the insert itself as the lock. So overlapping ticks cannot
    double-send, and a meeting moved in the calendar keeps its id and is not
    re-announced.

    Hours rather than all day: a nudge at 03:00 for an 03:30 event is noise, and
    nobody here holds external meetings outside the working day. Weekdays only,
    matching the note and the digest.
  -->
  <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
